Rule: wl075
  E1=0.950 (orig→recon), E2=0.050 (recon→orig)
  E_min=0.050, C_max=0.433, Score=-0.382
  Original:      No person may place a crab trap in public water unless a crab trap tag is attached to the trap unless a proclamation under Subchapter B , Chapter 78 , requires a license that does not require the use of crab trap tags.
  Reconstructed: A person who uses a crab trap on a public water body must ensure that the trap has an attached tag, unless the person has subchapter authorization and the trap does not have an attached tag.

Wait, let me re-analyze the formula more carefully.

The formula states:

If (crab trap) ∧ (uses device) ∧ (on public water) ∧ ¬(has_subchapter_authorization ∧ ¬has_attached_tag) → has_attached_tag

The negated condition `¬(has_subchapter_authorization ∧ ¬has_attached_tag)` is equivalent to `(¬has_subchapter_authorization ∨ has_attached_tag)`.

So the antecedent requires: crab trap ∧ uses ∧ public water ∧ (¬has_subchapter_authorization ∨ has_attached_tag).

Case 1: If has_attached_tag is true, the consequent is trivially satisfied.
Case 2: If has_attached_tag is false, then the antecedent requires ¬has_subchapter_authorization. In that case, the consequent demands has_attached_tag — contradiction. So has_attached_tag must be true.
Case 3: If has_subchapter_authorization is true and has_attached_tag is false, the inner negation fails, so the antecedent is false — no constraint.

So effectively: a person using a crab trap on public water must have an attached tag on the trap, unless the person has subchapter authorization.

A person who uses a crab trap on a public water body must ensure that the trap has an attached tag, unless the person has subchapter authorization.
  Rationale: Low mutual entailment (E_min=0.05), no clear relationship; asymmetric: forward stronger (0.95 vs 0.05)

